  <page>
    <title>Joseph Fry</title>
    <id>179805</id>
    <revision>
      <id>38929290</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-09T16:08:14Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Erp</username>
        <id>254824</id>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Joseph Fry''' (1728-1787) [[apothecary]] was the founder of [[J. S. Fry &amp; Sons]] [[chocolate]] company though it was called Fry, Vaughan &amp; Co. at that time.  He also founded Fry and Pine later Joseph Fry &amp; Co., a [[type setting|typefoundry]].  

He was the son of Joseph Fry (?-1775), a shopkeeper of Sutton Benger, Wiltshire, and apprenticed to Henry Portsmouth of Basingstoke as an apothecary and doctor  He married Portsmouth's daughter, Anna.

After his death in 1787 his wife, [[Anna Fry]] (1719/20-1803), took over the chocolate company and it was renamed Anna Fry &amp; Son.  The son was [[Joseph Storrs Fry (1769-1835)]] who, after his mother's death, renamed the firm [[J. S. Fry &amp; Sons]].  His son, Joseph Fry, was the father of [[Joseph Storrs Fry (1826-1913)]] who headed the firm as it gradually lost out to Cadburys.  

Joseph Fry &amp; Co., the typefoundry, was continued by Joseph Fry's son, [[Edmund Fry (1754-1835)]], and renamed Edmund Fry &amp; Co.. 

The second Joseph Fry was also the father of Sir [[Edward Fry]] (1826-1913), a judge on the British Court of Appeal.  Edward Fry was the father of the artist [[Roger Fry]] and the social reformers, [[Joan Mary Fry]] (1862-1955) and [[Margery Fry]] (1874-1958).

The first Joseph Fry's brother, William Storrs Fry, is the father of the Joseph Fry (1777-1861) who married the reformer [[Elizabeth Fry]].  There is also a William Storrs Fry who is the son of Joseph and Elizabeth Fry.  


[[Category:1728 births|Fry, Joseph]]
[[Category:1787 deaths|Fry, Joseph]]
{{business-bio-stub}}</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
