      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Rex E. Lee''' ([[February 27]], [[1935]] – [[March 11]], [[1996]]) from [[St. Johns, Arizona]] was a respected [[United States Constitution|Constitutional]] [[lawyer]], a [[Latter-day Saint]] (LDS; see also [[Mormon]]), an [[alumni|alumnus]] and tenth president of [[Brigham Young University]] from [[July 1]], [[1989]] through [[December 31]], [[1995]], [[law clerk|clerked]] for former [[U.S. Supreme Court of the United States|U.S. Supreme Court]] [[Justice]] [[Byron White]] and served as the [[United States Solicitor General]] under the [[Ronald Reagan|Reagan Administration]]. He argued 59 cases before the U.S. Supreme Court.  

He was a descendant of [[John D. Lee]] and [[Jacob Hamblin]] and a cousin to a number of [[Udall family]] politicians.

Current U.S. Supreme Court justice [[Samuel Alito]] served as assistant to Solicitor General Lee from 1981 to 1985, where Alito argued 12 cases before the U.S. Supreme Court.

==References==
*Rex E. Lee, ''A Lawyer Looks at the Constitution''; Brigham Young University Press; ISBN 0-8425-1904-1 (Softcover, September 1981)
*Rex E. Lee, ''What Do Mormons Believe''; Deseret Books; ISBN 0-8757-9639-7 (Hardcover, November 1992)

==External links==
*A Concurrent Resolution on the Death of Rex E. Lee by the Arizona Legislature:
**http://www.azleg.state.az.us/legtext/42leg/2r/laws/scr1018.htm
*Supreme Court Justices Pay Tribute to the Late Rex E. Lee:
**http://magazine.byu.edu/bym/1996/96november/lee.html
*Loving Rex Lee, A Personal Remembrance:
**http://magazine.byu.edu/bym/1996/96march/smemoriam.html

